<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/foundational/classification-tree-of-life">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Open a rainforest canopy or a drop of pond water and you find more kinds of living thing
    than anyone could name from memory. Biology needs a filing system — but not just any
    filing system. A librarian can shelve books by colour; biology shuffles its collection
    constantly, because species evolve, split and die out. What modern classification needs
    is a system that reflects <strong>actual ancestry</strong> — not convenience — so that
    grouping something tells you what it <em>is</em>, not just what it looks like. Modern
    classification is therefore a family tree (a <em>phylogeny</em>): species group by
    shared descent, and every named rank — domain, kingdom, phylum, class, order, family,
    genus, species — marks a branch point on it. "A whale is a mammal" is not a label; it is
    a hypothesis about who the whale's cousins are, and it correctly predicts milk, lungs,
    live birth and DNA.
  </p>
  <p>
    Before anything can be filed, though, biology must say what qualifies for a drawer. So
    this lesson builds in order: first what makes something alive at all, then how the
    filing works and what it is for, then the features that sort organisms into their major
    groups — ending with the revolution (told in the widget below) that replaced the old
    ladder of life with the branching tree we use today.
  </p>

  <h2>What makes something alive: the seven characteristics</h2>
  <p>
    A crystal grows, a candle moves and consumes fuel, a river takes in water — yet none is
    alive. Biology's answer is a checklist, not a single criterion: living things do
    <strong>all</strong> of seven things, memorably summarised as MRS GREN.
    <strong>Movement</strong> — of the whole organism or, in plants, of internal
    substances. <strong>Respiration</strong> — releasing energy from food by chemical
    oxidation (not "breathing", which is one way of feeding it). <strong>Sensitivity</strong>
    — detecting and responding to stimuli, from a sunflower tracking light to a bacterium
    swimming toward sugar. <strong>Growth</strong> — a permanent increase in size by making
    new material, not just swelling. <strong>Reproduction</strong> — producing new
    individuals, sexually, asexually, or both. <strong>Excretion</strong> — getting rid of
    the waste that metabolism creates (CO₂, urea), which is why un-excreted metabolic
    poisons kill. <strong>Nutrition</strong> — taking in or making organic molecules:
    by eating (animals), absorbing (fungi) or photosynthesising (plants). A
    <strong>virus</strong> fails most of the list — it does not respire, excrete or grow;
    it is a packaged set of instructions that hijacks other cells' machinery — which is why
    biologists class it as non-living, and why "is a virus alive?" is a useful exam in
    exactly what the checklist means.
  </p>

  <h2>Classification systems: the concept and the uses</h2>
  <p>
    Classification — <strong>taxonomy</strong> — proceeds through nested ranks. The
    <strong>species</strong> is the working unit: a group of organisms that can interbreed
    to produce fertile offspring (the definition strains with asexual microbes and hybrids,
    a wrinkle revisited below). Species group into <strong>genera</strong>, genera into
    families, then orders, classes, phyla (divisions for plants), kingdoms and
    <strong>domains</strong> — eight ranks, each more inclusive than the last. The naming
    is Linnaeus's lasting gift (1753): a two-part <strong>binomial</strong> —
    <em>Homo sapiens</em>, <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>Panthera leo</em> — genus first,
    species second, italicised, and identical in every language on Earth. Before binomials,
    a single plant might carry descriptive strings a paragraph long, changing from author
    to author; one standard name made science language-independent.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Why classify at all?</em> Four uses. <strong>Identification:</strong> with a
    <em>dichotomous key</em> — a forked questionnaire of visible features ("does it have
    legs? → does it have wings?") — anyone can name an unknown organism without an expert.
    <strong>Prediction:</strong> classification is a hypothesis machine — if a new fungus
    groups with penicillin's relatives, you can predict what else it might produce.
    <strong>Communication and record:</strong> conservation, agriculture and medicine all
    run on being able to say exactly which organism was involved. <strong>Evolutionary
    insight:</strong> a classification built on ancestry is a map of history — the only
    filing system whose drawers also tell you when and from what they branched.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked <strong>dichotomous key</strong> shows the identification use concretely.
    Four woodland animals, one key: 1a — has feathers → go to 2; 1b — has fur → go to 3.
    2a — webbed feet → duck; 2b — grasping feet → owl. 3a — tail flattened and scaly →
    beaver; 3b — tail bushy → squirrel. Each fork tests one visible character, and after
    log₂(4)-ish questions (two here, three for eight species — the arithmetic is why keys
    scale) an unknown lands on a name with no expert in the room. Keys are the
    user-facing half of classification; the tree is the theory half, and they are
    deliberately independent — a key can use any handy character, because its job is
    routing, not ancestry.
  </p>

  <h2>Features of organisms: the major groups</h2>
  <p>
    The classical kingdoms sort life by cellular architecture. <strong>Prokaryotes</strong>
    (bacteria and archaea) are single cells with no nucleus and no membrane-bound
    organelles; their DNA sits in a loop in the cytoplasm, their cell walls contain
    (in bacteria) peptidoglycan, and they reproduce by binary fission in as little as 20
    minutes. <strong>Protoctists</strong> are the eukaryotic odds-and-ends drawer: mostly
    single-celled organisms with true nuclei — <em>Amoeba</em>, <em>Chlorella</em>, the
    malaria parasite <em>Plasmodium</em>. <strong>Fungi</strong> are eukaryotes that cannot
    photosynthesise: they feed by <em>absorbing</em> decayed material through
    hyphae-threaded bodies (mushrooms are only their fruiting bodies), with cell walls of
    chitin — the fact, noted below, that moved them far from plants. <strong>Plants</strong>
    are multicellular photosynthesisers with cellulose walls, chloroplasts and starch as
    their store — from mosses (no vascular tissue) through ferns to conifers and flowering
    plants, whose flowers, seeds and fruits are the group's takeover technology.
    <strong>Animals</strong> are multicellular heterotrophs with no cell walls: they ingest
    food into a body cavity, move with muscles, coordinate with nerves, and divide into
    vertebrates (with backbones: fish, amphibians, reptiles, birds, mammals — fur and milk
    for the last) and the great spineless majority (insects, crustaceans, molluscs,
    annelids, cnidarians). The visible features — cell walls or none, chloroplasts or none,
    body plans — remain the everyday sorting tool of field biology, even though the
    <em>tree itself</em> is now drawn from DNA.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from ladder to tree — the four facts that reorganised all of life" widget="equationStep" config={treeCfg} />

  <h2>Reading the tree of life</h2>
  <p>
    The widget compresses the revolution; here is how to use the result. A
    <strong>cladogram</strong> is a branching diagram in which every fork is a inferred
    common ancestor and the tips are living groups. Three reading rules unlock it. Closer
    fork = closer relative. A group is a genuine evolutionary unit (a <em>clade</em>) only
    if it contains an ancestor and <em>all</em> of its descendants — "reptiles" excluding
    birds is a legacy of the old ladder, since crocodiles are closer kin to birds than to
    lizards. And every living species is equally "evolved": a bacterium is not a stopped
    primitive ancestor but a cousin with 4 billion years of its own refinement — the
    ladder's "simple to complex" ranking was Victorian vanity, not biology.
  </p>
  <p>
    The scale, once the tree is drawn, is humbling. Around 2.1 million species have been
    formally described; best estimates put the total at 8–10 million, most of them insects
    and other arthropods. By gene count, biomass and sheer cells, most of the biosphere is
    bacterial and archaeal: plants and animals are an evolutionary afterthought with
    excellent public relations. And extinction is part of the tree too — more than 99% of
    the species that ever lived are gone, their branches ending in fossils. The current
    extinction rate, running at hundreds of times the geological background, is pruning
    branches faster than we can name them: a third of the tree's twigs may vanish this
    century undocumented.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The method behind the modern tree is molecular comparison. DNA and protein sequences
    accumulate mutations at roughly steady rates, so the number of differences between two
    species' genes is a <strong>molecular clock</strong> — calibrate it against fossils of
    known age and it dates branch points where no fossils exist. Cytochrome c, a small
    respiratory protein, differs between human and chimpanzee in zero amino acids, between
    human and horse by ~12, human and yeast by ~45 — a ladder of kinship measured in a
    single molecule before genome sequencing existed. Today, DNA barcoding (a standard
    short gene region, COI for animals) identifies species from tissue scrapings, and
    environmental DNA from a spoonful of seawater lists the fish that swam past —
    taxonomy now partly happens in a centrifuge.
  </p>
  <p>
    Honest complications keep the field lively. The <strong>species problem</strong>: the
    interbreeding definition fails for asexual bacteria (most of life), for ring species
    (neighbouring populations interbreed but the ends do not), and for the 10–20% of plant
    and animal species that hybridise; working biologists fall back on diagnosable
    distinctness or on "evolutionarily independent lineages". <strong>Horizontal gene
    transfer</strong> — bacteria swapping genes across lineages — means the deepest parts
    of the tree are less a tree than a woven thicket, which is why the three-domain root
    (who branched first, bacteria or archaea) remains contested. And the <strong>rank
    problem</strong>: ranks are human conveniences, not natural units — "family" in birds
    and "family" in beetles are not comparable spans of time or diversity. The tree is
    real; the drawers are ours.
  </p>
  <p>
    Finally, the cladistics vocabulary that the modern tree is written in: a
    <strong>clade</strong> is defined by <strong>shared derived characters</strong>
    (synapomorphies) — feathers define the bird clade, mammary glands the mammal clade —
    and the method's discipline is distinguishing <em>derived</em> similarities (inherited
    from a recent common ancestor) from <em>ancestral</em> ones (inherited from a deeper
    ancestor, and therefore uninformative about close kin) and from <em>analogies</em>
    (wings of birds and butterflies — similar by function, unrelated by history).
    Convergent evolution is the trap the method exists to disarm: dolphins and sharks
    share a silhouette but not a lineage, and only the derived molecular and skeletal
    characters separate the mammal from the fish. Every claimed branch point on the tree
    of life is, in the end, a claim that some character is genuinely shared- derived —
    which is why the whole edifice now rests on DNA, the longest character list we have.
  </p>
